content.thinking:
Very interesting! From `block-to-block-grid-paste-translator-BDrE9DpJ.js`:

```js
return {
  contentData: e.contentData,
  settingsData: e.settingsData,
  expose: [],
  layout: {
    [r]: e.layout?.map((n) => ({
      ...n,
      columnSpan: 12,
      rowSpan: 1,
      areas: []
    }))
  }
};
```

So the block grid value has `layout: { [editorAlias]: [ ...items ] }` — the layout is an OBJECT keyed by editor alias, whose value is an ARRAY of layout items (not `{ items: [...] }`). In v15-v17 it was `{ "Umbraco.BlockGrid": { "items": [...] } }`? Hmm wait — actually no. Let me recall v13/14 BlockGrid value:

```json
"layout": {
  "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
    { "contentKey": "...", "settingsKey": "...", "columnSpan": 12, "rowSpan": 1 }
  ]
}
```

Hmm, actually in v13/14 the BlockGrid layout WAS `{ "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [ ...items... ] }`? No wait... Let me think. In v13/14:

BlockList value: `{ "layout": { "Umbraco.BlockList": [ { "contentKey": ..., "settingsKey": ..., "contentUdi"?... } ] }, "contentData": [...], "settingsData": [...] }`.

BlockGrid value (v13/14): `{ "layout": { "Umbraco.BlockGrid": { "items": [ { "contentKey": ..., "columnSpan": 12, "rowSpan": 1, "areas": [] } ] } }, ... }`.

Hmm, I genuinely am not sure whether v13/14 block grid layout was `{ "Umbraco.BlockGrid": { "items": [...] } }` or `{ "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [...] }`. The error message says: "Expected to find the beginning of an array of layout items for block editor alias: Umbraco.BlockGrid, got: StartObject." — so the deserializer, after reading the editor alias key ("Umbraco.BlockGrid"), expects an ARRAY but got an OBJECT (my `{ "items": [...] }`). So the correct v18 shape is:

```json
"layout": {
  "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
    { "contentKey": "...", "settingsKey": null, "columnSpan": 12, "rowSpan": 1, "areas": [] }
  ]
}
```

And the paste translator confirms: `layout: { [r]: e.layout?.map(...) }` where r is presumably the editor alias "Umbraco.BlockGrid", mapping items with columnSpan/rowSpan/areas.

So layout items have: contentKey, settingsKey, columnSpan, rowSpan, areas (array, for nested areas). Let me check the JS for the layout item shape more — from the paste translator: `{ ...n, columnSpan: 12, rowSpan: 1, areas: [] }`. So items have `areas` (empty array for simple blocks).

Let me verify by finding where the backoffice constructs a new block grid value from scratch — search for `contentData: [` near `layout:` in the block package. Or find the type definitions in the .d.ts? The bundle has .js.map files — source maps might contain the original TS types! Let me check a .map for "BlockGridValue" or "layout" types. Actually simpler: let me look at the Umbraco source on GitHub — default branch is "main" (hmm, interesting — Umbraco-CMS default branch is "main"? The API said default_branch: main. Wait, really? Umbraco-CMS... the fetch said "main". Hmm, but their version branches are like "v18.x.x"? Whatever — main should have the latest v18/v19 dev source. Since the project uses 18.2.0, let me fetch the v18.2.x branch or the tag. Let me list branches matching 18.

Actually, let me just fetch the JsonBlockValueConverter source from main and check DeserializeAndSetLayout to get the exact expected JSON. Path guess: src/Umbraco.Infrastructure/Serialization/JsonBlockValueConverter.cs. In v16+ the folders were renamed: src/Umbraco.Infrastructure (was Umbraco.Cms.Infrastructure). Let me fetch:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/umbraco/Umbraco-CMS/main/src/Umbraco.Infrastructure/Serialization/JsonBlockValueConverter.cs

And BlockGridValue: src/Umbraco.Core/Models/Blocks/BlockGridValue.cs.

Let me fetch both.

