Model shapes confirmed:
- BlogPost: Blocks (BlockGridModel), FeaturedImage (MediaWithCrops), PublishDate (DateTime), Summary (string)
- BlogFrontPage: Blocks (BlockGridModel)
- HeroBlock: BackgroundImage (MediaWithCrops), Heading (string), Lede (string)
- RichTextBlock: Body (IHtmlEncodedString)
- ImageBlock: Caption (string), Image (MediaWithCrops)
- QuoteBlock: Attribution (string), Quote (string)

No RazorRuntimeCompilation package in the csproj → views are compiled at build time (default Razor SDK behavior). So I must build after writing views, before restart. Plan:
1. TaskStop the site (bxipi61ct).
2. Write all files (models, view components, adapter partials, component views, page views, layout, _ViewImports edit).
3. dotnet build (compiles generated models + my code + Razor views — catches errors).
4. npm install + npm run build (frontend) — can run in background in parallel with step 3? npm and dotnet are independent. But running two heavy builds in parallel on Windows is fine. Let me run npm install in background first, then dotnet build foreground, then npm run build.
5. Start site, curl verify.

Also need to check the SiteRoot generated model? Not strictly needed for views. Skip.

Wait — one thing to verify: the friendly `Children<BlogPost>()` extension. It's in Umbraco.Extensions (FriendlyPublishedContentExtensions). The _ViewImports.cshtml already has @using Umbraco.Extensions. Good. I need to add @using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.

Also `GetCropUrl` on MediaWithCrops — friendly extension in Umbraco.Extensions (FriendlyMediaExtensions?). MediaWithCrops has GetCropUrl extension methods in Umbraco.Extensions namespace (ImageCropperTemplateExtensions / FriendlyImageCropperTemplateExtensions?). In AGENTS.md: `media.GetCropUrl(width: 800, height: 450)` — "MediaWithCrops or IPublishedContent" — friendly ones in Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common. With @using Umbraco.Extensions in _ViewImports, these resolve.

For BlockGridItem<T>: namespace Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks. The adapter partial: `@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlockGridItem<HeroBlock>>`. UmbracoViewPage is in Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.Views (already in _ViewImports). BlockGridItem needs @using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks — I'll add it in the adapter partials directly or in _ViewImports. AGENTS.md example adapter doesn't show a using for it... it shows just @inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlockGridItem<HeroBlock>>. Hmm, maybe BlockGridItem is in Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks and needs a using. I'll add `@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks` to _ViewImports to be safe. Wait — will that conflict with anything? No.

Actually, in Umbraco 13+, `BlockGridItem<T>` is in namespace `Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks`. Yes. Add the using.

ViewComponent discovery: ViewComponents must be in the app assembly; default discovery scans the entry assembly. Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco is the entry assembly — fine. Views/Shared/Components/<Name>/Default.cshtml — MVC default lookup. Good.

Now — where do ViewComponents live? AGENTS.md says "Each block is a ViewComponent" and shows `public sealed class HeroViewComponent : ViewComponent`. Conventions section says "One composer per concern in Composers/; services in Services/; notification handlers in Notifications/; migrations in Migrations/". It doesn't say where ViewComponents go. Common Umbraco convention: `ViewComponents/` folder at project root. The summary's plan says "ViewComponents/ folder with HeroViewComponent/...". I'll put them in Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco/ViewComponents/.

Plain view models: Models/Blocks/ — HeroModel, RichTextModel, ImageBlockModel, QuoteModel. Namespace follows folder: Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks.

Wait — namespace collision: Models/Blocks/ namespace Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks vs generated Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.HeroBlock — no collision. But careful: in adapter partials I'll have both `Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models` (generated blocks) and `Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks` (plain models). Class names differ (HeroBlock vs HeroModel) so no ambiguity.

Now the views:

**Views/BlogFrontPage.cshtml** — Umbraco view for blogFrontPage. In Umbraco 13+, the view name must match the template alias or the document type alias. Template alias: I created templates — what are their aliases? The blogFrontPage template id c1cadc23-0647-4402-abe2-4af7eb0c09e2 and blogPost template 5967b4c3-dcdd-46d5-9d36-03947764b79f. I need their aliases to name the views. Umbraco view resolution: Views/{templateAlias}.cshtml, falling back to Views/{contentTypeAlias}.cshtml. I should check the template aliases via MCP (get-template). Let me fetch both templates to get aliases.

